> However, if the aim is to devise a generic approach such that "a" was
> just one of the names that an object called col.name takes, that
> approach will not work.  

That was not the question.  You *can* use substitute to achieve it by my
approach, of course, so it *will* work.

> But what does work (at least on Ver 3.4), is
> 
> xx[, col.name] <- I(as.character(xx[, col.name]))

That also works and is more general.  But the question was about a simple
way.

> It has something to do with the automatic tendency to convert to
> factors in dataframes.  I don't quite understand just why
> 
> xx$a <- as.character(xx$a) works only on the column and why 
> 
> xx[, "a"] <- as.character(xx[, "a"]) feels compelled to recognise it's
> a part of a dataframe and to do what it does with dataframes.
> 
> It makes no difference if the xx[[ col.name]] approach is used.

> Is there a simple explanation?

[<- has a data.frame method and $<- does not, so x$a uses the method for
lists which does not promote character vars to factors whereas the
data.frame methods do.

Finally, the S-PLUS version is crucial here.  The original example works
in 5.1, as does A <- "a"; xx[[A]] <- as.character(xx[[A]])

